    "q-104016": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104016",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227671/assets/9c49ba7f220352a8933159a854d6e311baaaf2b6ec1e1f6fbdb78988653a157f.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-3$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is also continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"II and III only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227671/assets/f9c5342a872b4fd0ad2783b00d4d170b05844dc057a462832bf0eed41f50ccf4.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
