    "q-48064": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48064",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227671/assets/0218a1e13a0504b20e802100f9e15877beeb2b565a49fe7cd01cf2a0cf0197ee.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$",
      "worked_solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
